---
import CaseLayout from '../../layouts/CaseLayout.astro';
import Diagram from '../../components/Diagram.astro';
import RecordDiagram from '../../components/visuals/RecordDiagram.astro';

const sections = [
  { id: 'problem', label: 'The problem' },
  { id: 'reality', label: 'Operational reality' },
  { id: 'built', label: 'What I built' },
  { id: 'how', label: 'How it works' },
  { id: 'why', label: 'Why it matters' },
  { id: 'learned', label: 'What I learned' },
];
---

<CaseLayout slug="bs-bagels-operations-portal" sections={sections}>
  <section id="problem">
    <h2><span class="n">01 / The problem</span>The knowledge exists. It just isn&rsquo;t anywhere.</h2>
    <p>
      A restaurant&rsquo;s operating knowledge is real and extremely detailed. Somebody knows how
      much seasoning goes on the tray, which order the closing tasks have to happen in, what the
      proofer does when the room is humid, and exactly what the sandwich is supposed to look like
      when it leaves the window.
    </p>
    <p>
      What that knowledge doesn&rsquo;t have is an address. It lives in a binder nobody opens, a PDF
      someone emailed two years ago, a group chat, a label on a shelf, and mostly in the heads of
      whoever has been there longest.
    </p>
    <p>
      That arrangement works right up until the moment it doesn&rsquo;t. Saturday, 7:15. Line to the
      door. New hire on the build station. The person who knows is off.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="reality">
    <h2><span class="n">02 / Operational reality</span>The failure has a shape, and it repeats.</h2>
    <p>Watch it happen a few dozen times and it&rsquo;s always the same sequence:</p>
    <ul class="bullets">
      <li>Someone doesn&rsquo;t know, so they ask the nearest busy person.</li>
      <li>That person answers from memory, at speed, and sometimes gets it wrong.</li>
      <li>The build goes out wrong, or the prep gets made wrong, or the step gets skipped.</li>
      <li>Nobody logs it. It costs product, time, and a little of the customer&rsquo;s patience.</li>
      <li>The next new hire learns the same wrong answer from the same shortcut.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      There are two knock-on effects that are easy to miss. The first is that training capacity is
      capped by how many experienced people you can spare from the line &mdash; so training quality
      is worst exactly when you&rsquo;re growing fastest. The second is language: kitchens are
      multilingual and operational documentation usually isn&rsquo;t, which means the written
      material that does exist is least reachable for some of the people who need it most.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="built">
    <h2><span class="n">03 / What I built</span>A portal for someone standing up, with one hand free.</h2>
    <p>
      A mobile-first internal operations portal for the shop. Not a wiki and not a folder of PDFs
      &mdash; something built for the physical situation it gets used in: on a phone, mid-shift, in
      a hurry, often with gloves on.
    </p>
    <p>It holds the operation:</p>
    <ul class="bullets">
      <li>Recipes and menu builds, with visual build cards</li>
      <li>Prep procedures and production steps</li>
      <li>SOPs, opening and closing procedures</li>
      <li>Cleaning procedures and standards</li>
      <li>Equipment information and troubleshooting</li>
      <li>Checklists, training material and new-hire onboarding</li>
      <li>A separate manager layer, and content in more than one language</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      All of it searchable. The organising principle is that it&rsquo;s structured around the
      questions people actually ask during a shift, not around how the business happens to be
      organised on paper.
    </p>

    <Diagram
      wide
      caption="One record, and the five decisions inside it. Most of the work is at this scale."
    >
      <RecordDiagram />
    </Diagram>
  </section>

  <section id="how">
    <h2><span class="n">04 / How it works</span>The decisions that made the difference.</h2>
    <ul class="rows">
      <li>
        <b>Search is the front door</b>
        People arrive with a question, not a location. Nobody wants to guess whether &ldquo;how long
        do the onions go in for&rdquo; is filed under Prep, Recipes or Procedures.
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>Show before you describe</b>
        Builds are visual. You cannot read a paragraph with gloves on and a line forming. A picture
        of the correct thing answers the question faster than any sentence about it.
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>One screen, one task</b>
        If a procedure needs scrolling and re-reading, it&rsquo;s written at the wrong length. Long
        procedures get broken into steps that each fit on a phone.
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>Written for the person doing it</b>
        Not for the person who wrote the SOP, and not for a health inspector. Second person, short
        sentences, present tense, no preamble.
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>Multilingual by construction</b>
        Content exists in more than one language from the start rather than getting translated once
        and drifting out of date. If the kitchen is bilingual, the documentation has to be.
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>Two audiences, two layers</b>
        Crew get what they need to do the job in front of them. Managers get the layer above it
        &mdash; standards, troubleshooting, the things they&rsquo;re accountable for &mdash; without
        putting it in everyone&rsquo;s way.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="why">
    <h2><span class="n">05 / Why it matters</span>Knowledge stops being a person.</h2>
    <p>
      When the operation is written down and reachable, a few things change at once. Training gets
      faster and more consistent because it stops depending on who happens to be scheduled. The
      answer is the same every time, which is most of what consistency actually is. And turnover
      stops resetting the business, because the thing that leaves with someone is their skill, not
      the company&rsquo;s memory.
    </p>
    <p>
      But the most valuable part wasn&rsquo;t the software at all. It was what writing it down
      exposed.
    </p>
    <p class="pull">
      Documenting the operation meant discovering that three people were doing the same task three
      different ways &mdash; and then having to decide which one was right.
    </p>
    <p>
      Nobody had been hiding anything. The disagreement had simply never been visible in one place
      before. Half the operational improvement came from the conversations that caused, not from the
      tool.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="learned">
    <h2><span class="n">06 / What I learned</span>Mostly about adoption.</h2>
    <ul class="rows">
      <li>
        <b>Adoption is a design problem</b>
        Not a rollout problem, and not a compliance problem. If the answer takes more than a few
        taps, people ask a coworker instead &mdash; and at that point you&rsquo;ve lost, no matter
        how good the content is.
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>Complete is not the same as correct</b>
        My first version of several procedures was too thorough to use. The right level of detail is
        the level someone can act on while the timer is running, not everything I know about it.
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>Writing it down is the intervention</b>
        The software didn&rsquo;t fix the inconsistency. The act of writing it down forced the
        decisions that fixed it.
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>The hardest content is the obvious content</b>
        Everything nobody thinks to explain because &ldquo;everyone knows that&rdquo; is exactly
        what a new hire doesn&rsquo;t know, and it&rsquo;s the last thing anyone remembers to write.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</CaseLayout>
